      <p class="_abstract">When using the static CPU Manager policy, you can explicitly define a list of CPUs that are reserved for critical system processes on specific nodes. Reserving CPUs for critical system processes can help ensure cluster stability.</p>
      <p>For example, on a system with 24 CPUs, you could reserve CPUs numbered 0 - 3 for the control plane allowing the compute nodes to use CPUs 4 - 23.</p>
      <h2 id="nodes-nodes-resources-cpus-reserve_nodes-nodes-resources-cpus" class="group scroll-mt-20">Reserving CPUs for nodes<a href="#nodes-nodes-resources-cpus-reserve_nodes-nodes-resources-cpus" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">You can explicitly define a list of CPUs that are reserved for critical system processes on specific nodes by creating a <code>KubeletConfig</code> custom resource (CR) to define the <code>reservedSystemCPUs</code> parameter. Reserving CPUs for critical system processes can help ensure cluster stability.</p>
      <p>This list supersedes the CPUs that might be reserved by using the <code>systemReserved</code> parameter.</p>
      <p>For more information on the <code>systemReserved</code> parameter, see "Allocating resources for nodes in an OpenShift Container Platform cluster".</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
